.TH tuprovider n 0.1 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuprovider
.PP
A storage\-provider interface: one small abstraction over every storage back end (local filesystem, ZIP, WebDAV, ...). For a consumer \-\- a directory tree, a file list \-\- everything is just a path with children; where the bytes actually live does not matter.
.SS Load
.CS
tcl::tm::path add /path/to/tclutils/lib/tm
package require tclutils::tuprovider
.CE
.PP
The \fBlocal\fR backend is built in. Other schemes register themselves when their package is loaded (\fBtuprovider::zip\fR, \fBtuprovider::dav\fR).
.SS "Open a provider"
.CS
set p [::tclutils::tuprovider open local]
set p [::tclutils::tuprovider open zip /path/archive.zip]
set p [::tclutils::tuprovider open dav https://host/dav/]
.CE
.PP
\fBopen $scheme ?args?\fR returns a provider object; extra args go to the backend. An unknown scheme is an error.
.SS "The interface"
.PP
A provider answers these operations. Each takes provider paths (opaque to the consumer) and returns entry dicts or bytes:
.TS
l l l.
Operation	Signature	Result
\fBlist\fR	\fBlist $path\fR	list of entry dicts, one level, on demand
\fBstat\fR	\fBstat $path\fR	one entry dict, or error if absent
\fBget\fR	\fBget $path\fR	file contents as \fBraw bytes\fR
\fBput\fR	\fBput $path $data\fR	write contents
\fBdelete\fR	\fBdelete $path\fR	remove
\fBmkdir\fR	\fBmkdir $path\fR	create a directory/collection
\fBmove\fR	\fBmove $from $to\fR	rename/move within the provider
\fBcopy\fR	\fBcopy $from $to\fR	copy within the provider
\fBcaps\fR	\fBcaps\fR	which of the above are supported
.TE
.PP
\fBget\fR returns \fBraw bytes\fR (no encoding applied); a text consumer decodes them itself. Copying \fIacross\fR providers is done at the application level with \fBget\fR+\fBput\fR, not with \fBcopy\fR (which is provider\-internal only).
.SS "Entry dicts"
.PP
\fBlist\fR and \fBstat\fR return dicts with at least:
.CS
name   the leaf name
path   the provider path (feed back into list/stat/get)
type   file | dir
size   byte size (files)
mtime  modification time (where the backend provides it)
.CE
.SS Capabilities
.PP
Not every backend can do everything \-\- a ZIP is read\-only, WebDAV has no copy/move. \fBcaps\fR reports the truth so a UI never offers an operation that would fail:
.CS
if {"put" in [$p caps]} { $p put $dest $bytes }
.CE
.PP
Capabilities are discovered by introspection: a method counts as supported when a class other than the \fBBase\fR class implements it. A backend therefore just inherits \fBBase\fR and overrides the operations it can do \-\- its \fBcaps\fR follow automatically, at any inheritance depth.
.SS "Public commands"
.IP \(bu
\fB::tclutils::tuprovider::open $scheme ?args?\fR \-\- create a provider.
.IP \(bu
\fB::tclutils::tuprovider::register $scheme $class\fR \-\- register a backend class under a scheme (backends call this on load).
.IP \(bu
\fB::tclutils::tuprovider::schemes\fR \-\- list the registered schemes.
.SS "Writing a backend"
.PP
Subclass \fB::tclutils::tuprovider::Base\fR, override the operations you support, and register the class:
.CS
oo::class create ::tclutils::tuprovider::MyBack {
    superclass ::tclutils::tuprovider::Base
    method list {path} { ... }
    method stat {path} { ... }
    method get  {path} { ... }
}
::tclutils::tuprovider::register myscheme ::tclutils::tuprovider::MyBack
.CE
.PP
Only the overridden operations appear in \fBcaps\fR; the rest inherit \fBBase\fR's "not supported" default.
.SS "See also"
.PP
\fBtuprovider::zip\fR, \fBtuprovider::dav\fR
